"skill-retro", "skill-security-auditor", "skill-tester", "skillopt-sleep", "slo-architect", "snowflake-development", "soc2-audit-prep", "social-content", "social-media-analyzer", "social-media-manager", "spawn", "spec-driven-workflow", "spec-to-repo", "sql-database-assistant", "stage-launch", "stock-analysis", "strategic-alignment", "stress-test", "strict-api", "stripe-integration-expert", "tc-tracker", "tdd-guide", "team-communications", "tech-debt-tracker", "tech-stack-evaluator", "testrail", "threat-detection", "ui-design-system", "universal-scraping-architect", "ux-researcher-designer", "vendor-management", "vpe-review", "wrap-up", "zero-hallucination-coder", "zz-sql-review-20013", "design", "design-sync", "dataviz", "update-config", "verify", "debug", "code-review", "simplify", "batch", "fewer-permission-prompts", "doctor", "loop", "schedule", "claude-api", "workflow-authoring", "run", "run-skill-generator", "plugin-authoring"], "tests_pass": false, "base": "743c411cd9a74d42ca29812712bddef890fe1bf3", "capture": {"requests": 2, "leaks": [], "system_hashes": ["bf95ca34c568"], "tool_sets": ["ff763d4b64c6"], "request_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ratelimit": {"anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-status": "rejected", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-representative-claim": "seven_day", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-status": "allowed", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-fallback-percentage": "0.5", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-disabled-reason": "org_level_disabled", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-utilization": "0.29", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-status": "allowed"}, "probe_hit": false}}, "trace": {"commands": [], "calls": [["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/03f37e6346/workbench/query.sql"]], "final_message": "The query in `query.sql` has three performance problems:\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n1. **`lower(u.email)` can't use a normal index.** Wrapping the column in a function means an index on `users(email)` is ignored, so the database scans the whole `users` table. Pick one of these fixes:\n   - Create an expression index: `CREATE INDEX users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));`. This works in Postgres, and MySQL 8+ supports functional indexes too.\n   - Store emails already lowercased and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain index.\n   - Use a case-insensitive type or collation, such as `citext` in Postgres or a `_ci` collation in MySQL. A plain index then works.\n\n2. **`SELECT *` returns every column from both tables.** That costs extra I/O and network transfer. It also prevents index-only scans, and the join duplicates the user columns on every order row. List only the columns you need, for example `o.id, o.total, o.created_at`.\n\n3. **`orders.user_id` may have no index.** If it isn't indexed, each matching user triggers a full scan of `orders`. Check with `CREATE INDEX orders_user_id_idx ON orders (user_id);`. Many databases don't create this automatically for foreign keys. Postgres doesn't, and MySQL/InnoDB does.\n\nSmaller points:\n- **No `LIMIT` or pagination:** a user with many orders returns all of them.\n- **Unique email:** if emails are unique, which is likely, the lookup is a single-row fetch once the index exists. In that case the join is cheap, and fixing 1 and 3 is enough.\n- **Unconfirmed:** I only saw this one query, not the schema or row counts. Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after the changes to confirm the effect.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}